; exit 70 (see .exit)
; An allocation the kernel refuses, and the sentence the program says on
; its way out.
;
; `MM-ALLOC-7` records that allocation failure exits 70 and that there is
; no recoverable out-of-memory condition. What it did not record - and
; what this case exists to pin - is that the status used to arrive ALONE.
; Both `oom:` labels in the emitted allocator ended in a bare `exit(70)`,
; so a program that ran out of memory vanished with a number and no
; output at all.
;
; For a compiler that is survivable: the operator is watching it run. For
; the long-lived programs this language is now used to write it is not.
; A worker in a pre-forked pool disappears, the supervisor respawns it,
; and nothing anywhere says why - which is the difference between a
; production incident somebody can diagnose and one they cannot.
;
; THE SIZE IS 2^60 AND THAT IS NOT ARBITRARY. macOS overcommits: a
; request for 1 TiB SUCCEEDS here and the failure path is never reached,
; so a smaller number would make this case pass by not testing anything.
; The size has to be past the user address space on every target this
; compiler emits for, so that the mapping is refused rather than merely
; unbacked - and 2^47, which this case asked for until 2026-08-29, is
; not: FreeBSD 14.4/arm64 has 48 bits of user address space and
; accounts no overcommit (`vm.overcommit` 0), so it GRANTED the
; mapping and this case exited 0 with no message. 2^60 is past all six
; targets' address spaces.
;
; The message is pinned in `314-out-of-memory.err` and the status in
; `.exit`. Neither is checked by the other: a program that printed the
; right sentence and exited 0 would fail on the status, and one that
; exited 70 in silence - which is what this tree did until now - fails
; on the stderr.
(import IO)

(import Mem)

(:: main Int)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (main)
  {
    (printlnLit (strCStr "asking for more than the address space holds"))
    (memAlloc 1152921504606846976)
    ; NOT REACHED, and this line is deliberately the TAIL of `main`.
    ; `verify-stdlib-out.py` models a case's exit status from that tail
    ; when it is a bare numeral, so a `0` there is a claim that this
    ; program returns 0 - which it does not, because it never returns at
    ; all. A non-literal tail is how a case says "my status does not come
    ; from my last expression", and that is the truth here: it comes from
    ; the allocator, and `.exit` is where it is written down.
    ;
    ; It is also the tripwire for the other half of MM-ALLOC-7. If the
    ; allocator ever starts ANSWERING instead of exiting - which needs a
    ; primitive and therefore a moved seed - this line runs, stdout gains
    ; a line, and the golden says so.
    (printlnLit (strCStr "UNREACHABLE: the allocation was satisfied"))
  }
)
